# Miscellaneous Benign Breast Conditions

## Breast Cysts

- **Definition** - fluid-filled, epithelial lined cavities within breast parenchyma with varying size from microscopic to large palpable masses
- **Epidemiology:**
  - <u>Prevalence</u> - Palpable cyst develops in 1 in 14 women
  - <u>Risk of recurrence</u> - 50% of recurrence are multiple or recurrent
- **Pathophysiology** - related to destruction and dilatation of lobules and terminal ductules, with <u>influence by ovarian hormones</u>
- **Clinical features** - incidental finding or palpable mass (confirmed on USG or aspiration)
  - <u>Never in post-menopausal women</u> - new cyst formation in post-menopausal women only occur in a/w exogenous HRT
- **Ix and Dx:**
  - **USG** - differentiation between simple or complex cyst:
    - <u>Sonographic features of simple cysts</u> - homogenous anechoic smooth-bordered lesion with posterior acoustic enhancement
    - <u>Sonographic features of complex cyst</u> - heterogenous echogenicity (e.g. mixture of anechoic areas with echoic foci)
  - **Direct aspiration and cytology** - if complex on USG
- **Risk of malignancy** - cystic carcinoma are extremely rare (\< 0.1%)
- **Mx** - surgical removal of cyst only if recurrent or if needle Bx shows atypiia, or if symptomatic

## Fibroadenomas

- **Definition** - benign solid tumors of the breast with stromal and epithelial components
- **Epidemiology** - 2nd most common tumor of the breast (following CA):
  - <u>Incidence</u> - dependent on age group:
    - \< 30y - most common tumor (most often arise in late teens and early reproductive years)
    - \> 40-45y - unlikely for new masses to be fibroadenomas
- **Clinical features:**
  - <u>Palpable mass</u> - firm, lobulated or smooth mobile mass that may increase rapidly in size over several mo, and waxes and wanes w/ menstrual cycle
- **Signs** - palpable, extremely mobile mass
- **Dx** - USG can readily distinguish between cysts and fibroadenomas abd Dx is made on histology
- **Risk of malignancy** - neoplasia can develop from epithelial components (neoplasia at presentation \< 0.2%):
  - LCIS (pre-malignant according to AJCC) - 50%
  - Invasive carcinoma - 35%
  - Intraductal carcinoma - 15%
- **Mx** - open excisional biopsy or via percutaneous approach (only if bothered by mass or growing)

