## Papillomas and Papillomatosis

- **Papillomas** - true polyps of epithelial-lined breast ducts with a fibrovascular core
- **Papillomatosis** - epithelial hyperplasia not composed of true papillomas but no stalk of fibrovascular tissue

## Sclerosis Adenosis

- **Definition** - histological lesion characterised by increased number of small terminal ductules or acina in association with proliferation of stromal tissue
- **Clinical significance** - associated with deposition of calcium, which is indistinguishable with microcalcifications of invasive breast carcinoma on mammography
- **Risk of malignancy** - no significant malignancy potential

## Radial Scars

- **Definition** - complex sclerosing lesions with microcysts, epithelial hyperplasia and adenosis and a prominent display of central sclerosis
- **Clinical features of radial scar:**
  - <u>Breast mass</u> - gross abnormality is rarely \> 1cm, but large radial scars can form palpable masses and appear as spiculated mass on radiography
  - <u>Skin changes</u> - skin dimpling may be produced by traction on surrounding tissue
- **Dx** - Bx or excision required to r/o underlying malignancy
- **Risk of malignancy** - moderate increased risk for breast cancer

## Fat Necrosis

- **Definition** - fat necrosis of adipose tissues in the breast precipitated by trauma to a prior surgical procedure or radiation therapy
- **Clinical features of fat necrosis** - palpable mass that may contain calcifications
- **Risk of malignancy** - no malignant poteention
